t use this information.\n 37 | #\n 38 | # To begin, we'll visualize our data.\n 39 | \n 40 | # Generate the class/group data\n 41 | n_points = 100\n 42 | X = np.random.randn(100, 10)\n 43 | \n 44 | percentiles_classes = [.1, .3, .6]\n 45 | y = np.hstack([[ii] * int(100 * perc)\n 46 |                for ii, perc in enumerate(percentiles_classes)])\n 47 | \n 48 | # Evenly spaced groups repeated once\n 49 | groups = np.hstack([[ii] * 10 for ii in range(10)])\n```\n\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | from .base import _get_response\n  2 | \n  3 | from .. import auc\n  4 | from .. import roc_curve\n  5 | \n  6 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  7 | \n  8 | \n  9 | class RocCurveDisplay:\n 10 |     \"\"\"ROC Curve visualization.\n 11 | \n 12 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.plot_roc_curve` to create a\n 13 |     visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 14 | \n 15 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 16 | \n 17 |     Parameters\n 18 |     ----------\n 19 |     fpr : ndarray\n 20 |         False positive rate.\n 21 | \n 22 |     tpr : ndarray\n 23 |         True positive rate.\n 24 | \n 25 |     roc_auc : float, default=None\n 26 |         Area under ROC curve. If None, the roc_auc score is not shown.\n 27 | \n 28 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 29 |         Name of estimator. If None, the estimator name is not shown.\n 30 | \n 31 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 32 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing the roc auc\n 33 |         metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is considered\n 34 |         as the positive class.\n 35 | \n 36 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 37 | \n 38 |     Attributes\n 39 |     ----------\n 40 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 41 |         ROC Curve.\n 42 | \n 43 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 44 |         Axes with ROC Curve.\n 45 | \n 46 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 47 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 48 | \n 49 |     See Also\n 50 |     --------\n 51 |     roc_curve : Compute Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n 52 |     plot_roc_curve : Plot Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n 53 |     roc_auc_score : Compute the area under the ROC curve.\n 54 | \n 55 |     Examples\n 56 |     --------\n 57 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 58 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn import metrics\n 60 |     >>> y = np.array([0, 0, 1, 1])\n 61 |     >>> pred = np.array([0.1, 0.4, 0.35, 0.8])\n 62 |     >>> fpr, tpr, thresholds = metrics.roc_curve(y, pred)\n 63 |     >>> roc_auc = metrics.auc(fpr, tpr)\n 64 |     >>> display = metrics.RocCurveDisplay(fpr=fpr, tpr=tpr, roc_auc=roc_auc,\\\n 65 |                                           estimator_name='example estimator')\n 66 |     >>> display.plot()\n 67 |     <...>\n 68 |     >>> plt.show()\n 69 |     \"\"\"\n 70 | \n 71 |     def __init__(self, *, fpr, tpr, roc_auc=None, estimator_name=None, pos_label=None):\n 72 |         self.estimator_name = estimator_name\n 73 |         self.fpr = fpr\n 74 |         self.tpr = tpr\n 75 |         self.roc_auc = roc_auc\n 76 |         self.pos_label = pos_label\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay` is incomplete; search `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay` for the full content if needed.\n\nFound `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | import scipy as sp\n  2 | \n  3 | from .base import _get_response\n  4 | \n  5 | from .. import det_curve\n  6 | from .._base import _check_pos_label_consistency\n  7 | \n  8 | from ...utils import check_matplotlib_support\n  9 | from ...utils import deprecated\n 10 | \n 11 | \n 12 | class DetCurveDisplay:\n 13 |     \"\"\"DET curve visualization.\n 14 | \n 15 |     It is recommend to use :func:`~sklearn.metrics.DetCurveDisplay.from_estimator`\n 16 |     or :func:`~sklearn.metrics.DetCurveDisplay.from_predictions` to create a\n 17 |     visualizer. All parameters are stored as attributes.\n 18 | \n 19 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n 20 | \n 21 |     .. versionadded:: 0.24\n 22 | \n 23 |     Parameters\n 24 |     ----------\n 25 |     fpr : ndarray\n 26 |         False positive rate.\n 27 | \n 28 |     fnr : ndarray\n 29 |         False negative rate.\n 30 | \n 31 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 32 |         Name of estimator. If None, the estimator name is not shown.\n 33 | \n 34 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n 35 |         The label of the positive class.\n 36 | \n 37 |     Attributes\n 38 |     ----------\n 39 |     line_ : matplotlib Artist\n 40 |         DET Curve.\n 41 | \n 42 |     ax_ : matplotlib Axes\n 43 |         Axes with DET Curve.\n 44 | \n 45 |     figure_ : matplotlib Figure\n 46 |         Figure containing the curve.\n 47 | \n 48 |     See Also\n 49 |     --------\n 50 |     det_curve : Compute error rates for different probability thresholds.\n 51 |     DetCurveDisplay.from_estimator : Plot DET curve given an estimator and\n 52 |         some data.\n 53 |     DetCurveDisplay.from_predictions : Plot DET curve given the true and\n 54 |         predicted labels.\n 55 | \n 56 |     Examples\n 57 |     --------\n 58 |     >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 59 |     >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n 60 |     >>> from sklearn.metrics import det_curve, DetCurveDisplay\n 61 |     >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n 62 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n 63 |     >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=1000, random_state=0)\n 64 |     >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n 65 |     ...     X, y, test_size=0.4, random_state=0)\n 66 |     >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)\n 67