he styles applied to the current Styler.\n2076 | \n2077 |         Can be applied to a second Styler with ``Styler.use``.\n2078 | \n2079 |         Returns\n2080 |         -------\n2081 |         styles : dict\n2082 | \n2083 |         See Also\n2084 |         --------\n2085 |         Styler.use: Set the styles on the current Styler.\n2086 |         Styler.copy: Create a copy of the current Styler.\n2087 | \n2088 |         Notes\n2089 |         -----\n2090 |         This method is designed to copy non-data dependent attributes of\n2091 |         one Styler to another. It differs from ``Styler.copy`` where data and\n2092 |         data dependent attributes are also copied.\n2093 | \n2094 |         The following items are exported since they are not generally data dependent:\n2095 | \n2096 |           - Styling functions added by the ``apply`` and ``applymap``\n2097 |           - Whether axes and names are hidden from the display, if unambiguous.\n2098 |           - Table attributes\n2099 |           - Table styles\n2100 | \n2101 |         The following attributes are considered data dependent and therefore not\n2102 |         exported:\n2103 | \n2104 |           - Caption\n2105 |           - UUID\n2106 |           - Tooltips\n2107 |           - Any hidden rows or columns identified by Index labels\n2108 |           - Any formatting applied using ``Styler.format``\n2109 |           - Any CSS classes added using ``Styler.set_td_classes``\n2110 | \n2111 |         Examples\n2112 |         --------\n2113 | \n2114 |         >>> styler = DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]]).style\n2115 |         >>> styler2 = DataFrame([[9, 9, 9]]).style\n2116 |         >>> styler.hide(axis=0).highlight_max(axis=1)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2117 |         >>> export = styler.export()\n2118 |         >>> styler2.use(export)  # doctest: +SKIP\n2119 |         \"\"\"\n2120 |         return {\n2121 |             \"apply\": copy.copy(self._todo),\n2122 |             \"table_attributes\": self.table_attributes,\n212